# G072 — File Downloader

*v2. See `file-downloader.md` for prose: disk is the durable state,
atomic rename as promotion primitive, resume requires hash to cover
already-downloaded bytes, transport as injected parameter, hash-
mismatch keeps .partial for forensics.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Vault Media Ingestion with Integrity

[ingest-remote-media @url
    IN ORDER TO
        download a media file survivably — atomic, resumable, hash-verified
        and flag corruption rather than silently accept wrong bytes

    @dest          <- [vault[media-path {url: @url}]]
    @expected-hash <- [manifest[lookup-hash {url: @url}]]

    @outcome <- [download[fetch
                           {transport: [http[ranged {url: @url}]],
                            dest: @dest,
                            opts: {resume: true,
                                    expected-hash: @expected-hash}}]]
        || where resistance is hash-mismatch
            -> [log[write
                     {level: "error",
                      url: @url, mismatch: @resistance}]]
               [manifest[flag-corrupt {url: @url}]]
               retry with fresh start

    @outcome
        where verified is true
            -> [vault[register-media
                       {path: @dest, hash: @expected-hash}]]
        otherwise
            -> #integrity-failed {url: @url}
]

---

## Verb Surface

[download
    fetch            {transport, dest, opts, on-progress?}
    ;; opts.resume, opts.chunk-size, opts.expected-hash
]

[http
    ranged           {url}                    ;; -> Transport
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: atomic promotion `where` bytes verify

[download[fetch {transport: @t, dest: @dest, opts: @o}]
    IN ORDER TO
        write bytes to @dest.partial durably
        then atomically rename to @dest only if the hash verifies
        so observers of @dest see absence-or-complete, never partial

    bytes stream into @dest.partial via @t starting at size(@dest.partial)
    hasher seeded from @dest.partial on resume
    on completion:
        where hash equals @o:expected-hash
            -> rename @dest.partial to @dest (atomic on POSIX)
        otherwise
            -> keep @dest.partial for inspection (do NOT delete)
               -> #hash-mismatch
]

## Distinctive Innate idiom: transport as injected parameter

[download[fetch {transport: @t, ...}]
    @t is any value that answers [read-range {offset, length}]
    where production uses http-ranged
    and tests use an in-memory fake that can fail at specific offsets
]

Same discipline every testable networked system uses. The downloader
code is identical between production and test; only the transport
changes.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @t <- [fake-transport[new {bytes: "...1MB payload..."}]]
    [fake-transport[fail-at {transport: @t, offset: 500000}]]

    @outcome <- [download[fetch
                           {transport: @t,
                            dest: "/tmp/media.bin",
                            opts: {resume: true,
                                    expected-hash: 0xDEADBEEF}}]]
    ;; first attempt writes 500000 bytes to /tmp/media.bin.partial then fails

    [fake-transport[clear-failures {transport: @t}]]
    @outcome <- [download[fetch
                           {transport: @t,
                            dest: "/tmp/media.bin",
                            opts: {resume: true,
                                    expected-hash: 0xDEADBEEF}}]]
    ;; resume picks up at byte 500000, hasher seeded from .partial

    -> @outcome:verified                    ;; -> true
    -> [fs[exists {path: "/tmp/media.bin"}]] ;; -> true
    -> [fs[exists {path: "/tmp/media.bin.partial"}]] ;; -> false
]
